# Godrick Pendlebrook's camp talks (story/camp_talk.gd): offered after a Long Rest once the opening `if` holds; each
# plays once. Quest: A Knight, More or Less (narrative/companions/ladle.dialogue). The file offers its first talk
# that holds, so the approval talks come in this order (story/approval.gd): when he's Strained or Doubtful, those
# first; then the quest's; then Warm, Close and Devoted as the campaign goes on.

# --- After Strahd called Godrick to kneel at the night visit (strahd/visits:night_kneel) ------------------------------

~ talk_knelt
if name:godrick_pendlebrook and flag.strahd_knelt == "godrick_pendlebrook"
-> knelt
endif
-> END

~ knelt
Narrator: Godrick has tied his sword to his wrist with a bootlace. He has been sitting up with it like that since before dawn.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: He called me squire, and I got up. Just like that. Like it was Sir Pellam calling.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I left my sword in the grass. A knight doesn't leave his sword. A knight doesn't kneel to... I knelt.
* Your oath is still yours, Godrick. -> knelt_oath
* The bootlace is a good idea. -> knelt_lace

~ knelt_oath
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Then I'll say it every night, out loud, so it's the last thing in my head when I sleep. You'll have to put up with that.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: It's quite long. Sir Pellam added bits.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +3: You told me my oath was still mine
-> END

~ knelt_lace
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: It's daft. I know it's daft. Next time he calls me, he can have the sword too, and see how he likes the end of it.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +2: You let me keep my sword tied to my wrist
-> END

# --- When he's lost faith in the party (approval) ----------------------------------------------------------------

~ talk_strained
if name:godrick_pendlebrook and approval.godrick_pendlebrook <= strained
-> strained
endif
-> END

~ strained
Narrator: Godrick takes his bedroll to the far side of the fire tonight and sits on it with his back very straight, and doesn't laugh at anything.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I'm going to say this once, and then I'll carry on, because I said I would.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I came here to be a knight. A proper one. And I've been standing next to things a knight doesn't stand next to.
if flag.death_house_sacrificed
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: We put one of our own on a table in the dark. I still see it when I shut my eyes.
elif flag.dark_gifts_taken >= 1
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: We let one of us take a gift from a thing in a box. A devil's gift. Kip's told us what those cost.
elif flag.gertruda_given_back
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: We sent a frightened girl back up the stairs to him. I promised her mother. I promised.
endif
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I'll fight beside you. I'll not leave you. But when it comes to the Order and the oath, I can't kneel and say those words with this on me.
* Then help us do better, Godrick. Tell us when we're wrong. -> strained_help
* We're sorry. Truly. -> strained_sorry
* Then don't kneel. -> strained_cold

~ strained_help
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I can do that. I'm good at saying when things are wrong. I'm loud. It's one of my gifts.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Right. We start again tomorrow. Everybody gets to start again tomorrow. That's what tomorrow's for.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +10: You asked me to tell you when we're wrong
-> END

~ strained_sorry
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Sorry's a start. Sir Pellam said sorry's the door and not the house. We'll see about the house.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +6: You said you were sorry, and meant it
-> END

~ strained_cold
Narrator: He nods slowly, as if you've told him something he already knew, and lies down with his back to the fire.
approve godrick_pendlebrook -5: You told me not to kneel, then
-> END

~ talk_doubt
if name:godrick_pendlebrook and approval.godrick_pendlebrook == doubtful
-> doubt
endif
-> END

~ doubt
Narrator: Godrick has been quiet all evening, which for Godrick is like a mountain being quiet. At last he puts down the sword he's been sharpening and clears his throat.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Can I say something? I'm no good at hard things, so I'll say it plain and then I'll stop.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Sir Pellam said a knight's only as good as the people he won't leave behind. Lately I keep looking back, and I keep seeing people we left.
if flag.bonegrinder_bargain == "abandoned" or flag.den_children_left
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Children. We walked away from children in a cage. I keep hearing it.
endif
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I'm not saying I'm better than anybody. I'm not clever enough to be sure what's right. But I'm sure about some things, and that's one of them.
* You're right. We'll do better. -> doubt_better
* We did what we had to, Godrick. -> doubt_had_to
* You don't have to come with us. -> doubt_go

~ doubt_better
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Right then. That's all I wanted. Well, no. I wanted it not to have happened. But that's the next best thing.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +6: You heard me out when I said we'd been leaving people behind
-> END

~ doubt_had_to
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Maybe. You're cleverer than me; maybe you can see a way it had to be. I can't. I'll keep looking for it.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +1: You told me we did what we had to
-> END

~ doubt_go
Narrator: For a moment he looks as though you'd struck him. Then the cheer goes out of his face entirely, and he speaks very gently.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I do, though. That's the thing about an oath. I swore I'd see this through. I never swore I'd like how.
approve godrick_pendlebrook -4: You told me I didn't have to come with you
-> END

# --- His quest ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

~ talk_ladle
if name:godrick_pendlebrook and quest.the_ladle
-> ladle
endif
-> END

~ ladle
Narrator: Godrick has the old sword across his knees and is polishing a part of it that is already very clean. Beside him on the log, also being polished, is a soup ladle.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: You'll be wondering about the ladle.
* We were, yes. -> ladle_story
* (Sit down and wait.) -> ladle_story

~ ladle_story
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Sir Pellam knighted me with it. On his deathbed. In an inn called the Wheatsheaf, which did a very good mutton soup, hence the ladle.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Twelve years I carried his shield and mucked out his horse and listened to him talk about honour. He never knighted me. I asked him why once. He said I wasn't ready. I asked when I'd be ready. He said he'd know.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: And then at the very end he says, "Kneel, lad," and he can't lift the sword, so he picks up the ladle off his soup tray and does it with that. And then he says, "Now take the sword to the Order and have them do it proper." And then he died.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: So here's my question, and I want a straight answer, because I'm not clever and I can't work it out on my own. Does a ladle count?
* It counts. -> ladle_counts
* That's what the Order is for. We'll find them. -> ladle_order
* He knew you were ready. That's the part that counts. -> ladle_ready

~ ladle_counts
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: That's what I think too, most days. Then I think, it's a ladle. Then I think, it was his ladle. Round and round.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +1: You told me the ladle counts
-> ladle_end

~ ladle_order
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Right. Yes. Find the Order, hand over the sword, kneel. Simple. I'm good at simple.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +1: You promised we'd find the Order
-> ladle_end

~ ladle_ready
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: ...Huh.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I'm going to have to sit with that one a while. I'm slow with the big ones. I get there.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +3: You told me Sir Pellam knew I was ready
-> ladle_end

~ ladle_end
interject name:liriel_dawnsong: The Morninglord was crowned with a loaf of bread in the old stories. A ladle seems perfectly respectable.
interject name:thistle: It's a good ladle. I'd be knighted with that. Can you eat soup with it after? Then it's two things. That's better than a sword.
interject name:wren_featherfoot: I've been knighted, you know. Well. Called a knight. By a magistrate. It wasn't a compliment.
interject name:ratatoille: May I see it? This is a very good ladle. Copper-bottomed. Somebody loved this ladle.
interject name:kip_smudgewick: If you ever want to sell it... no. No, sorry. Force of habit. Never sell that.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Right then. Bed. Big day tomorrow. All the days are big here. Have you noticed? It's very tiring.
-> END

~ talk_letters
if name:godrick_pendlebrook and quest.the_ladle >= the_order_found and not (quest.the_ladle >= knighted) and flag.eva_truth_teller != "godrick_pendlebrook"
-> letters
endif
-> END

~ letters
Narrator: Godrick is sitting with a page of somebody's old book open on his knee, frowning at it in the firelight as if it had insulted his mother.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Can I tell you something? You won't laugh? You can laugh a bit. Not a lot.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I can't read. Not properly. My name, and "ale", and "danger", because Sir Pellam said those were the three a knight needs. The rest of it swims about.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Every letter we've had, I've handed to someone and said my eyes are tired. My eyes are fine. They're the best eyes in the party.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: And now there's an Order, and they'll have books, and oaths written down, and I'll stand there like a great big post.
* We'll teach you. Starting tonight. -> letters_teach
* A knight's oath is what you do, not what you read. -> letters_do
* Thank you for telling us. -> letters_thanks

~ letters_teach
set godrick_letters
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Tonight? Right now? I've got the page here. I've been holding it for an hour hoping it'd give in.
Narrator: It takes most of the watch. It's mostly the K in "knight", which he takes personally.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Why is it there? What's it doing? It's not doing anything! Who let it in?
if name:ratatoille
interject name:ratatoille: It's silent. Like the p in... no, don't, that'll make it worse.
endif
if name:wren_featherfoot
interject name:wren_featherfoot: It's there to keep the rest of the word in order. Like a sergeant. A silent sergeant.
endif
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: K. N. I. G. H. T. Knight. ...I read that. That was me reading.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +4: You sat up half the night teaching me to read
-> END

~ letters_do
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Sir Pellam used to say that. I always thought he was being kind. Maybe he was being right. He could be both. He was good at both.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +2: You told me an oath is what you do, not what you read
-> END

~ letters_thanks
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Thank you for not making a thing of it. That's a kindness. I'm keeping a list of kindnesses. Well. Somebody else is writing it down.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +2: You didn't make a thing of it when I told you I can't read
-> END

~ talk_knighted
if name:godrick_pendlebrook and quest.the_ladle >= knighted
-> knighted
endif
-> END

~ knighted
Narrator: Sir Godrick doesn't polish anything tonight. He sits with the ladle in both hands and looks at it a long while.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I keep thinking, I should hang it up somewhere. Over a fireplace. That's what knights do with things. But I haven't got a fireplace. I've got a log.
if flag.godrick_letters
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Sir Godfrey gave me the oath on a bit of paper, after. I can read most of it. Not "sleeper". Sleeper's got two Es together, that's showing off.
endif
if name:ratatoille
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Here. Rat. You have it. You'll use it right. Sir Pellam liked soup more than he liked honour, he told me once, and he liked honour more than anyone I ever met.
interject name:ratatoille: I... no. I couldn't. It's your... thank you. I'll make soup with it. The best soup. Every time, I'll make the best soup.
else
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I'll keep it. Somebody'll need soup one day. A knight's for people who need things.
endif
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Right then. That's done. Now there's only the vampire.
-> END

# --- As he comes to trust the party (approval) --------------------------------------------------------------------

~ talk_warm
if name:godrick_pendlebrook and approval.godrick_pendlebrook >= warm and level >= 3
-> warm
endif
-> END

~ warm
Narrator: Godrick is trying to whittle. It is going badly. Something that might once have been a horse is now mostly a stick with a sad face.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: It's a horse. Was a horse. It's a dog now. It'll be a spoon by the end.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Can I tell you something daft? Back home I was the youngest of six. The others were clever, or quick, or good with money, and I was large. That was my whole job. Being large, and carrying things.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: You lot ask me what I think. In a fight, in a town. You wait for the answer, even when it's slow coming. Nobody's waited for my answer before.
* Your answers are worth waiting for. -> warm_worth
* You're more than large, Godrick. -> warm_more
* (Take the knife and show him how.) -> warm_knife

~ warm_worth
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Ha! Now you're just being kind. ...Keep being kind. I like it.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +2: You told me my answers are worth waiting for
-> END

~ warm_more
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I'm also tall. ...That was a joke. I make jokes now. Wren's a bad influence.
if name:wren_featherfoot
interject name:wren_featherfoot: It was a terrible joke. I've never been prouder.
endif
approve godrick_pendlebrook +2: You told me I'm more than large
-> END

~ warm_knife
Narrator: You take the knife and the stick and show him: thumb behind the blade, little cuts, away from yourself. He watches with his tongue between his teeth. By the end of the watch the dog is a spoon, and a fairly good one.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: A spoon! I meant a spoon. I definitely meant a spoon the whole time.
if name:ratatoille
interject name:ratatoille: It's a beautiful spoon. Lopsided. Lopsided spoons stir better; everyone knows that.
endif
approve godrick_pendlebrook +3: You showed me how to whittle, and didn't laugh at the dog
-> END

~ talk_close
if name:godrick_pendlebrook and approval.godrick_pendlebrook >= close and level >= 5
-> close
endif
-> END

~ close
Narrator: Godrick sits up on watch long after his turn is over, looking out at nothing. When you come to relieve him, he doesn't move over. He just says it.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I'm scared of something. Not monsters. Monsters are easy; you hit them.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I'm scared people only like me because I'm big and I smile a lot. Like a good dog. And if I stopped smiling, or got hurt and couldn't carry things, they'd find somebody else to like.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I've never said that out loud. It sounds worse out loud.
* We'd still be here if you couldn't lift a cup. -> close_stay
* Godrick, we like you because you're good. -> close_good
* Everybody's afraid of that. -> close_everyone

~ close_stay
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: ...You would, wouldn't you. I can tell. I'm good at telling. It's the one clever thing I've got.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +3: You told me you'd stay even if I couldn't carry anything
-> close_end

~ close_good
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Good. Huh. Sir Pellam said that once, on his last day. I thought he was rambling.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +3: You told me you like me because I'm good
-> close_end

~ close_everyone
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Are they? Even Wren? Even Liriel? Huh. That's a comfort, and a bit sad. Mostly a comfort.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +2: You told me everybody's afraid of that
-> close_end

~ close_end
Narrator: He gets up at last, stretches until his back cracks like a fence post, and puts one enormous hand on your shoulder, very gently, as if you might break.
if flag.godrick_letters
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Right then. Bed. That's going on the list of kindnesses. I'll write it down myself. I can do that now. Badly.
else
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Right then. Bed. That's going on the list of kindnesses. Somebody'll have to write it down for me.
endif
-> END

~ talk_devoted
if name:godrick_pendlebrook and approval.godrick_pendlebrook >= devoted and level >= 8
-> devoted
endif
-> END

~ devoted
Narrator: Tonight Godrick has the ladle out again. Not polishing it. Just holding it, the way you'd hold a letter from home.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: I've been thinking what Sir Pellam would make of you lot. He was very particular about company. He sent a bishop away once for slurping.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: He'd like you. He'd argue with every one of you, and then he'd like you. That's how you knew he liked you; he argued.
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: When this is done, if we're alive, you're coming to the manor. All of you. There's more sheep than tenants and the roof leaks in the good bedroom, but there's room. There's always been more room than people.
* We'll come. -> devoted_yes
* Will there be soup? -> devoted_soup

~ devoted_yes
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Right then. That's settled. I'll tell Mum. She'll cry. She cries at everything; it's where I get it.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +3: You promised to come to the manor, after
-> devoted_end

~ devoted_soup
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: There'll be soup. I'll make it myself, with the ladle. Ha! It'd finally be doing two things at once.
approve godrick_pendlebrook +3: You asked whether there'd be soup at the manor
-> devoted_end

~ devoted_end
interject name:godrick_pendlebrook: Whatever's up at that castle, I'm not afraid of it. I never was, much; I'm not clever enough. But now I've got a better reason. I've got you lot to walk back down with.
inspire name:godrick_pendlebrook: knew what he was fighting for
-> END
